<worksheet>
    <title>
         Laplace transform for the Heaviside function
    </title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            
            <li>
                <p>
                    The Heaviside function geometrically and algebraically.
                </p>
            </li>
<li>
    <p>
        Laplace transform for the Heaviside function.
    </p>
</li>
   <li>
    <p>
        Using Laplace transform to solve differential equations with the heaviside function.
    </p>
   </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
           Let <m>a\ge 0</m>, the Heavyside function
                       <fn>
                           Named after the Eng.
                           Oliver Heayvside
                       </fn>, 
                       denoted by <m>u_{a}(t)</m> is defined by
                       <md>
                           u_{a}(t)=\left\{ \begin{array}{lr}0, &amp; \text{if }t&lt;a;\\ 1, &amp; \text{if } t\ge a.\end{array} \right.
                       </md> 
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<example>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Find the Laplace transform of the Heaviside function <m>\mathscr{L}\{u_{a}(t)\}</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            We have
            <md>
                \mathscr{L}\{u_{a}(t)\}=\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-st}u_{a}(t)dt=\int_{a}^{\infty}e^{-st}dt=\left[-\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\right]_{a}^{\infty}=\frac{1}{s}e^{-as}.
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<section>
<title>Shifting the origin on the <m>t-</m>Axis</title>
<introduction>
    <sidebyside>
        <p>
            Given a function <m>f(t)</m>, suppose that we wish to consider a function
                    <m>g(t)</m>, which is the same as the function <m>f (t)</m>, but shifted so that
                    time <m>t = 0</m> for <m>f</m> corresponds to some later time, say <m>t = a</m>,
                    for <m>g</m>.
                    For time <m>t &lt; a</m> we let <m>g(t) = 0</m>.
                    (So <m>g</m> is the same as <m>f</m> except that <m>g</m> is <q>turned on</q> at
                    time <m>t = a</m>.) An efficient way to write <m>g(t)</m> is
                    <md>
                        g(t)=u_{a}(t)\cdot f(t-a)
                    </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            <image source="13_1.jpg" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graph of<m>f(t)=e^{-t}</m> and <m>g(t)=u_3(t)\cdot e^{-(t-3)}</m>.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
        </p>
    </sidebyside>
</introduction>
<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
        If <m>\mathscr{L}\{f(t)\}=F(s)</m> then
         <md>
        \mathscr{L}\{ f(t-a)\cdot u_a(t)\} = e^{-as}\cdot F(s).   
        </md>
           Similarly, if <md>\mathscr{L}^{-1}\{e^{-as}\cdot F(s)\}=f(t-a)\cdot u_a(t)</md> then

        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<example>
    <introduction><p>Find the Laplace transform of the following functions</p></introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\mathscr{L}\{ (t+4)\cdot u_3(t)\}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\mathscr{L}\{ (t+4)^2\cdot u_3(t)\}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </task>


    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\mathscr{L}\{ e^{t+4}\cdot u_3(t)\}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </task>
</example>
<example>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Find the inverse Laplace transform of the following functions
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\displaystyle{\mathscr{L}^{-1}\left\{41e^{-3s}\frac{1}{s^5}\right\}}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        </task>

         <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\displaystyle{\mathscr{L}^{-1}\left\{5e^{-4s}\frac{1}{s+4}\right\}}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        </task>

         <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\displaystyle{\mathscr{L}^{-1}\left\{e^{-s}\frac{14}{(3s+2)(s-4)}\right\}}</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
        </task>
</example>

    </section>

<section>
<title>Using Laplace to solve DE with the Heavyside function</title>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Solve the following initial value problem using Laplace transform
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>y'=-y+u_1(t)\cdot (t-1),  \mbox{ and  } \quad y(0)=2.</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>y'=-2y+u_2(t)\cdot e^{-t}, \mbox{ and  }\quad y(0)=3.</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
        
<sidebyside>
    <p>Consider the RC circuit with voltage source <md>V(t)=2u_{4}(t)\cdot e^{-(t-4)}</md>.</p>

            <image source="13_2.jpg" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The picture of an RC circuit and the graph of <m>V(t)=2u_{4}(t)e^{-(t-4)}</m>.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
</sidebyside>
    
    <p>
The voltage source turns on with a voltage 2 at time <m>t=4</m> and then decays
exponentially as <m>t</m> increases.
The differential equation for the voltage <m>v_{c}</m> across the capacitor is
<md>
    RC\frac{dv_{c}}{dt}+v_{c}=V(t).
</md>
Suppose that the initial voltage across the capacitor is <m>v_{c}(0)=5</m>. 
Taking the (unrealistic) values <m>R = 1</m> and <m>C = 1/3</m> and using
<m>V(t)</m> given above, we have the initial-value problem
<md>
    \frac{1}{3}\frac{dv_{c}}{dt}+v_{c}=2u_{4}(t)\cdot e^{-(t-4)},v_{c}(0)=5.
</md>
</p> </introduction>


   
</exercise>
    </section>
    </worksheet>